nodecode-deja is a rewrite of vshulcz's vshulcz/deja-vu:

  https://github.com/vshulcz/deja-vu
  commit 910d490f6b1909fa7a469c3adf95c0ec5d5187c5
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Vladislav Shulcz (LICENSE)

The rewrite is extensions/hermes, the plugin's Hermes memory provider, as a
Nodecode cell. What it keeps: the three tools with their names and their
descriptions (deja_recall, deja_fix, deja_blame); the argv each one builds —
the query after `--` when it names one of deja's own flags, `--framed` for
fix and blame, `--offset` only past the first page, and the "this deja cannot
page" answer when a paging request comes back empty; the automatic recall,
split as the plugin split it — the session digest at a session's first
request, the relevance pass over the turn's own prompt, nothing at all on a
cron run; deja's own JSON hook reply split by reader, with the
hookSpecificOutput context riding the request the model reads and the
systemMessage reaching the operator; and the standing paragraph that tells
the model when to reach for the history and that what comes back is data.

What the plugin got from Hermes and Nodecode has no seam for is left out: the
compression recovery packet (on_pre_compress with on_session_switch's
compression reason), the session-end stamp (on_session_end), and the
mirroring of MEMORY.md / USER.md entries into deja's index
(on_memory_write). Hermes's own recall line, and its rules for when a
platform shows one, are gone with it: the cell puts deja's line for the
person up as a notice instead.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell runs the same deja
program the plugin ran, on PATH, and its own code is Common Lisp.
